<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Vervellum is NOT sandboxed, and this file deliberately enables no App Sandbox
	  key. The optional "research the selection" feature reads the frontmost app's
	  selected text through the Accessibility API, and a sandboxed app can never hold
	  that permission — AXIsProcessTrusted() is always false and the app cannot even
	  be added in System Settings. That rules out the Mac App Store.

	  No other entitlement is needed: the global shortcut uses Carbon's
	  RegisterEventHotKey (permission-free), and outside the sandbox the Hardened
	  Runtime does not gate outgoing network connections.

	  WHAT CI ACTUALLY SHIPS: release.yml produces an **ad-hoc signed, unsigned,
	  un-notarized** build. Signing with codesign's ad-hoc identity ("-") is the minimum
	  required to launch on Apple Silicon; it is not a Developer ID signature, so
	  Gatekeeper warns on first
	  launch. ENABLE_HARDENED_RUNTIME applies to a locally signed build; the ad-hoc CI
	  build carries no embedded entitlements.

	  CONSEQUENCE, and it is a real one: TCC keys the Accessibility grant to the code
	  signature — the cdhash for an ad-hoc signature. An ad-hoc cdhash changes with
	  every build, so **every update silently invalidates the user's Accessibility
	  grant** and "research the selection" stops working until they re-grant it.
	  Vervellum detects this and says so rather than failing quietly. Moving to a
	  Developer ID signature (which TCC keys by Team ID + bundle ID instead) is the
	  fix, and is the top item in the backlog for that reason.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
